---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'Contact — HumanAverage';
const description =
  'Questions, corrections and source suggestions for HumanAverage. Demonstrable errors are ' +
  'fixed and the correction is noted.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} canonical="/contact/">
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Contact</p>
      <h1>Found a mistake? Tell me.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A site built on a promise of traceable numbers only works if the numbers can be
        challenged. Corrections are welcome and get fixed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow content">
      <h2>Email</h2>
      <p>
        <a class="button" href="mailto:firenature23@gmail.com">firenature23@gmail.com</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        There is no contact form, because a form would mean collecting your details on a site
        whose whole point is that it collects almost nothing.
      </p>

      <h2>What helps</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>About a specific result:</strong> the page, the country you chose, the value
          you entered and the answer you got back. That is enough to reproduce it exactly.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>About a figure you think is wrong:</strong> which number, and what you believe
          it should be. A source makes it far quicker to check — and if you are right, it gets
          corrected.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>About something not working:</strong> your device, browser and what you did
          just before it broke.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Suggesting a dataset:</strong> a link to the data itself, not to an article
          about it. What works best is a series an agency publishes per country and keeps
          updating.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What this is not</h2>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          HumanAverage cannot answer medical, psychological or personal health questions, and
          nothing here is a diagnosis. If a measurement worries you, that is a conversation for
          a doctor, who can see everything a country-level statistic cannot.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>
        Background on who runs this and how it is funded is on the
        <a href="/about/">about page</a>. How the numbers are produced is on the
        <a href="/method/">method page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
